$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 04/Case definitions

::EPI-W4-001::Which element should usually NOT be built into an outbreak case definition when it is the exposure you plan to test? {
=The hypothesised exposure#Correct. Embedding the exposure can manufacture an association.
~Clinical criteria#Case definitions may include clinical, time, place and person criteria; the tested exposure should remain independent.
~A relevant time restriction#Case definitions may include clinical, time, place and person criteria; the tested exposure should remain independent.
~A relevant place restriction#Case definitions may include clinical, time, place and person criteria; the tested exposure should remain independent.
}

::EPI-W4-002::What is the main purpose of a standard case definition in descriptive epidemiology? {
=To classify cases consistently#Correct. Consistent classification makes counts and comparisons interpretable.
~To prove the cause of disease#The central purpose is consistent classification, not causal proof.
~To maximise the number of variables collected#The central purpose is consistent classification, not causal proof.
~To replace laboratory testing in every setting#The central purpose is consistent classification, not causal proof.
}

$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 04/Time

::EPI-W4-003::Cases rise every rainy season at roughly the same months each year. Which description best fits? {
=Seasonal pattern#Repeated within-year timing is seasonality.
~Secular trend#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
~Point-source outbreak#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
~Random error only#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
}

::EPI-W4-004::A cancer mortality rate falls steadily over 25 years. Which description best fits? {
=Secular trend#Long-term directional change is a secular trend.
~Seasonal pattern#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
~Point-source pattern#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
~Person-to-person propagation#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
}

::EPI-W4-005::Most cases occur within one incubation period after a single shared meal. Which description best fits? {
=Point-source pattern#A compressed cluster within one incubation period is compatible with a point source.
~Secular trend#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
~Seasonal pattern#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
~Propagated pattern#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
}

::EPI-W4-006::Cases occur in successive waves separated by roughly one incubation period. Which description best fits? {
=Propagated pattern#Successive waves can suggest person-to-person spread.
~Point-source pattern#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
~Secular trend#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
~No temporal pattern#Choose the description that matches the time scale and shape.
}

::EPI-W4-007::A patient developed symptoms on day 2 but was reported on day 5. For an epidemic curve intended to approximate transmission timing, which date is generally more informative? {
=Date of symptom onset#Correct. Onset is usually closer to the biological event being described.
~Date the report was entered#Reporting dates can be distorted by administrative delay.
~Date the weekly report was printed#Reporting dates can be distorted by administrative delay.
~Either date is always identical#Reporting dates can be distorted by administrative delay.
}

