$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 04/Place

::EPI-W4-013::A map places one dot at the residence of every identified case. What type of display is this? {
=Spot map#Correct. A spot map shows individual event locations.
~Population pyramid#A spot map uses a marker for each case or event.
~Survival curve#A spot map uses a marker for each case or event.
~Frequency polygon#A spot map uses a marker for each case or event.
}

::EPI-W4-014::Two districts have 300 and 120 cases, but populations of 600,000 and 60,000 respectively. Which statement is most defensible? {
=The second district has the higher case rate#Correct. The rates are 0.5 versus 2.0 per 1,000.
~The first district has the higher case rate because it has more cases#Counts alone do not account for population size.
~The districts have identical frequency#Counts alone do not account for population size.
~Rates cannot be compared when counts differ#Counts alone do not account for population size.
}

::EPI-W4-015::Area A reports 100 cases in 100,000 people. Area B reports 80 cases in 50,000 people. Which area has the higher disease frequency based on population rate? {
=Area B#Correct. Compare cases relative to population size.
~Area A#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
~They are exactly equal#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
~Cannot be assessed with denominators provided#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
}

::EPI-W4-016::Area A reports 120 cases in 200,000 people. Area B reports 90 cases in 100,000 people. Which area has the higher disease frequency based on population rate? {
=Area B#Correct. Compare cases relative to population size.
~Area A#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
~They are exactly equal#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
~Cannot be assessed with denominators provided#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
}

::EPI-W4-017::Area A reports 140 cases in 300,000 people. Area B reports 100 cases in 150,000 people. Which area has the higher disease frequency based on population rate? {
=Area B#Correct. Compare cases relative to population size.
~Area A#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
~They are exactly equal#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
~Cannot be assessed with denominators provided#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
}

::EPI-W4-018::Area A reports 160 cases in 400,000 people. Area B reports 110 cases in 200,000 people. Which area has the higher disease frequency based on population rate? {
=Area B#Correct. Compare cases relative to population size.
~Area A#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
~They are exactly equal#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
~Cannot be assessed with denominators provided#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
}

::EPI-W4-019::Area A reports 180 cases in 500,000 people. Area B reports 120 cases in 250,000 people. Which area has the higher disease frequency based on population rate? {
=Area B#Correct. Compare cases relative to population size.
~Area A#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
~They are exactly equal#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
~Cannot be assessed with denominators provided#Calculate cases divided by the population for each area.
}

$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 04/Person

::EPI-W4-020::Why might a 0 to 4 year age category conceal an important pattern? {
=Risk may be concentrated within a narrower age band such as infants#Correct. Broad categories can average over important heterogeneity.
~Age can never be analysed categorically#Category width should preserve epidemiologically meaningful variation.
~All children under five have identical risk#Category width should preserve epidemiologically meaningful variation.
~Rates cannot be calculated by age#Category width should preserve epidemiologically meaningful variation.
}

::EPI-W4-021::Which is the strongest reason to examine disease frequency by occupation? {
=Occupation may proxy relevant exposures or conditions of work#Correct.
~Occupation proves causation#Person characteristics can suggest exposure hypotheses but do not themselves prove causes.
~Occupation replaces age and sex#Person characteristics can suggest exposure hypotheses but do not themselves prove causes.
~Occupational categories always have equal denominators#Person characteristics can suggest exposure hypotheses but do not themselves prove causes.
}

::EPI-W4-022::Most cases are concentrated among under 1 year. What is the most appropriate next descriptive step? {
=Calculate frequency using a denominator for that subgroup if available#Correct. Subgroup-specific denominators make the pattern interpretable.
~Conclude the subgroup characteristic caused disease#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
~Ignore the pattern until regression is fitted#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
~Use the total population denominator for every subgroup#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
}

::EPI-W4-023::Most cases are concentrated among adolescents. What is the most appropriate next descriptive step? {
=Calculate frequency using a denominator for that subgroup if available#Correct. Subgroup-specific denominators make the pattern interpretable.
~Conclude the subgroup characteristic caused disease#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
~Ignore the pattern until regression is fitted#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
~Use the total population denominator for every subgroup#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
}

::EPI-W4-024::Most cases are concentrated among adults aged 65 years or older. What is the most appropriate next descriptive step? {
=Calculate frequency using a denominator for that subgroup if available#Correct. Subgroup-specific denominators make the pattern interpretable.
~Conclude the subgroup characteristic caused disease#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
~Ignore the pattern until regression is fitted#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
~Use the total population denominator for every subgroup#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
}

::EPI-W4-025::Most cases are concentrated among health workers. What is the most appropriate next descriptive step? {
=Calculate frequency using a denominator for that subgroup if available#Correct. Subgroup-specific denominators make the pattern interpretable.
~Conclude the subgroup characteristic caused disease#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
~Ignore the pattern until regression is fitted#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
~Use the total population denominator for every subgroup#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
}

::EPI-W4-026::Most cases are concentrated among market vendors. What is the most appropriate next descriptive step? {
=Calculate frequency using a denominator for that subgroup if available#Correct. Subgroup-specific denominators make the pattern interpretable.
~Conclude the subgroup characteristic caused disease#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
~Ignore the pattern until regression is fitted#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
~Use the total population denominator for every subgroup#A count without the relevant population at risk can mislead.
}

$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 04/Counts and rates

::EPI-W4-027::Group 1 has 50 cases among 5,000 people; Group 2 has 80 cases among 20,000. Which has the higher rate? {
=Group 1#Correct. Rates compare event counts relative to population size.
~Group 2#Do not infer comparative frequency from counts alone.
~The group with more cases always has the higher rate#Do not infer comparative frequency from counts alone.
~There is no denominator information#Do not infer comparative frequency from counts alone.
}

