$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 04/Hypothesis generation

::EPI-W4-035::What is the usual relationship between descriptive and analytic epidemiology? {
=Descriptive patterns generate hypotheses that analytic comparisons can test#Correct.
~Descriptive epidemiology proves causation#Descriptive person-place-time patterns commonly generate testable hypotheses.
~Analytic epidemiology should always precede description#Descriptive person-place-time patterns commonly generate testable hypotheses.
~They are unrelated activities#Descriptive person-place-time patterns commonly generate testable hypotheses.
}

::EPI-W4-036::A cluster is concentrated near two water points after flooding. What is the best interpretation? {
=The pattern generates a water-related hypothesis that needs testing#Correct.
~The water points are proven causes#Pattern is a clue, not proof.
~Spatial clustering is always random#Pattern is a clue, not proof.
~No further comparison is useful#Pattern is a clue, not proof.
}

::EPI-W4-037::An outbreak shows a surprising case 1 incubation period later than most others. What should an investigator do first? {
=Verify the observation and explore whether it is an error, secondary case or different exposure#Correct. Outliers can be errors or important clues.
~Delete it automatically#Investigate surprising observations before excluding them.
~Assume it proves a second outbreak#Investigate surprising observations before excluding them.
~Ignore it because outliers are never useful#Investigate surprising observations before excluding them.
}

::EPI-W4-038::An outbreak shows a surprising case 2 incubation periods later than most others. What should an investigator do first? {
=Verify the observation and explore whether it is an error, secondary case or different exposure#Correct. Outliers can be errors or important clues.
~Delete it automatically#Investigate surprising observations before excluding them.
~Assume it proves a second outbreak#Investigate surprising observations before excluding them.
~Ignore it because outliers are never useful#Investigate surprising observations before excluding them.
}

