$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 04/Data quality

::EPI-W4-042::A new clinic started reporting during the same week that case counts doubled. What is the key interpretation issue? {
=The observation system changed, so the apparent increase may not be entirely biological#Correct.
~The clinic proves an outbreak occurred#Changes in reporting can change counts without equivalent changes in true disease.
~The population denominator becomes irrelevant#Changes in reporting can change counts without equivalent changes in true disease.
~No data-quality check is needed#Changes in reporting can change counts without equivalent changes in true disease.
}

::EPI-W4-043::Duplicate case reports most directly inflate which quantity? {
=The numerator#Correct.
~The population denominator#Duplicates add apparent cases.
~The incubation period#Duplicates add apparent cases.
~Specificity of the map#Duplicates add apparent cases.
}

::EPI-W4-044::Before plotting a line list, record 44 has a missing onset date. What is the most defensible action? {
=Flag and investigate the missing value before deciding how to use the record#Correct.
~Invent the median onset date#Missing data should be investigated and handling documented.
~Delete the entire case automatically#Missing data should be investigated and handling documented.
~Use report date without documenting the substitution#Missing data should be investigated and handling documented.
}

::EPI-W4-045::Before plotting a line list, record 45 has a missing onset date. What is the most defensible action? {
=Flag and investigate the missing value before deciding how to use the record#Correct.
~Invent the median onset date#Missing data should be investigated and handling documented.
~Delete the entire case automatically#Missing data should be investigated and handling documented.
~Use report date without documenting the substitution#Missing data should be investigated and handling documented.
}

